# Airy functions and Bessel functions of order `±1/3` (DLMF §§9.6, 9.7)

For real `x > 0` and `ζ = (2/3) x^{3/2}` (`airyZeta`, DLMF 9.6.1) we prove

* `airyAi_eq_besselI`, `airyAi_eq_besselK`: `Ai(x) = (√x/3)(I_{-1/3}(ζ) - I_{1/3}(ζ))
  = π⁻¹ √(x/3) K_{1/3}(ζ)` (9.6.2);
* `airyBi_eq_besselI`: `Bi(x) = √(x/3)(I_{1/3}(ζ) + I_{-1/3}(ζ))` (9.6.4);
* `airyAi_neg_eq_besselJ`: `Ai(-x) = (√x/3)(J_{1/3}(ζ) + J_{-1/3}(ζ))` (9.6.6);
* `airyBi_neg_eq_besselJ`: `Bi(-x) = √(x/3)(J_{-1/3}(ζ) - J_{1/3}(ζ))` (9.6.8);

and the leading terms of the large-`x` expansions (DLMF 9.7.5, 9.7.7):

* `airyAi_isEquivalent`: `Ai(x) ~ e^{-ζ}/(2√π x^{1/4})`;
* `airyBi_isEquivalent`: `Bi(x) ~ e^{ζ}/(√π x^{1/4})`;

and `airyAi_ofReal_pos`, `airyBi_ofReal_pos`: both functions are positive on `[0, ∞)`.

The asymptotics come from Hankel's expansion of `K_ν` (`besselK_ray_isBigO`) and the
large-argument behaviour of `I_ν` (`besselI_sub_exp_isBigO`).

# DLMF §§1.2–1.3 results already upstream

The `dlmf_upstream` commands below are checked pointers to Mathlib (and one TauCeti) declarations
for DLMF §§1.2–1.3 (elementary algebra, matrices, determinants, linear operators), deliberately
not duplicate local theorems. Conventions:

* the binomial coefficient of 1.2.1 is `Nat.choose`, the one of 1.2.6 for complex `z` is
  `Ring.choose`;
* matrices are `Matrix m n R`; DLMF's `Aᴴ` is `Matrix.conjTranspose`, `\overline{A}` is
  `A.map star`, `etr(A)` is `exp (trace A)`;
* DLMF's scalar product `⟨u, v⟩` (linear in `u`) is Mathlib's `⟪v, u⟫` (linear in the second
  argument), see `DLMF/AlgebraicAnalytic/Algebra/Matrices.lean`;
* the matrix norm 1.2.67 is the `l²` operator norm, a scoped instance
  (`open scoped Matrix.Norms.L2Operator`);
* Mathlib's `Matrix.adjugate` is the transposed cofactor matrix: `adjugate A k j = A_{jk}`.

# Calculus of one variable (DLMF §1.4)

Most of DLMF §1.4 is in Mathlib; the pointers are in
`DLMF.AlgebraicAnalytic.Calculus.MathlibCoverage`. This file states the remaining items:

* the ε–δ form 1.4.2 of right continuity;
* the antiderivatives 1.4.17 and the linear combination 1.4.19;
* improper integrals at a finite endpoint 1.4.23 and absolute convergence (§1.4(v));
* absolutely continuous and discrete Stieltjes measures 1.4.23_1–1.4.23_3;
* principal values of integrable functions (1.4.24–1.4.25);
* the first and second mean value theorems for integrals 1.4.29–1.4.30, repeated integrals
  1.4.31, the total variation formula 1.4.34;
* L'Hôpital's rule for `∞/∞` (1.4.14–1.4.15) and Faà di Bruno's formula 1.4.13;
* extrema on a closed interval (§1.4(vii)) and the derivative criteria for convexity
  (§1.4(viii)).

The integral mean-value formulas use Mathlib’s
`exists_eq_const_mul_intervalIntegral_of_nonneg` and
`exists_eq_const_mul_intervalIntegral_of_nonneg_of_monotoneOn`. The mixed Stieltjes measure
formula uses Mathlib’s `integral_add_measure`, `integral_finsetSum_measure`, and the integral
identity for a measure with density; the finite atomic measure is unchanged by restriction
because all its mass points lie inside the integration interval. L’Hôpital’s rule with an
unbounded denominator uses the Mathlib-based increment comparison theorem in
`DLMFMathlib.Analysis.Calculus.LHopitalInfinity`.

# Convergents of continued fractions (DLMF §§1.12(ii)–1.12(iv))

For `contFrac a b = b₀ + a₁/(b₁ + a₂/(b₂ + ⋯))` (DLMF indexing) with canonical numerators
`A_n = (contFrac a b).nums n` and denominators `B_n = (contFrac a b).dens n`:

* the recurrences 1.12.5–1.12.6 (with `A_{-1} = 1`, `B_{-1} = 0` as Mathlib's
  `contsAux 0 = ⟨1, 0⟩`), the nested form 1.12.4 of `C_n = A_n/B_n`;
* the determinant formula 1.12.7 and its consequences 1.12.8–1.12.14;
* equivalence transformations 1.12.15–1.12.17, Euler's series fraction 1.12.18–1.12.19;
* the fractional transformation 1.12.20–1.12.21;
* the characterization 1.12.22 of sequences of convergents (finite-valued form);
* even and odd parts 1.12.23–1.12.24.

The approximants are the ratios `A_n/B_n` (Mathlib's `GenContFract.convs`); the nested fractions
(`GenContFract.convs'`) agree with them when the nested denominators do not vanish (1.12.4).
Several statements are therefore stronger than DLMF's (no hypothesis `p_k ≠ 0` in 1.12.18).

# Distributions and the Dirac delta: definitions (DLMF §§1.16–1.17)

Definitions for DLMF §1.16 (distributions, tempered distributions, Fourier transforms of
tempered distributions) and §1.17 (delta sequences). Mathlib already provides the spaces:

* `𝓓'(Ω, F)` (`Distribution`): continuous `ℝ`-linear maps on the real test functions
  `𝓓(Ω, ℝ)` on an open set `Ω`, with values in `F`; complex distributions take `F = ℂ`
  (testing against real test functions only is equivalent, Mathlib's implementation notes);
* `𝓢'(E, F)` (`TemperedDistribution`): continuous `ℂ`-linear maps on the Schwartz space
  `𝓢(E, ℂ)`, with the topology of pointwise convergence;
* `Distribution.delta`, `TemperedDistribution.delta`, `Distribution.ofFun`, the distributional
  derivatives `∂_{v}`, the Fourier transform `𝓕` on `𝓢'` (Mathlib's convention
  `𝓕 f ξ = ∫ e^{-2πi⟪x, ξ⟫} f x dx`).

This file adds the DLMF objects that are not upstream:

* `heaviside` (1.16.13), `xPlusRpow` (1.16.19), `logPlus` (1.16.22);
* `stieltjesDistribution α` (1.16.3_5), the distribution `φ ↦ ∫ φ dα` of a Stieltjes function;
* `distribIterDeriv k T` (1.16.9), the `k`-th distributional derivative on the line;
* `xPlusDistrib α` (1.16.19, 1.16.21, 1.16.23), the distributions `x₊^α` for all real `α`;
* `dlmfFourierSchwartz` (1.16.29): DLMF's Fourier transform
  `𝓕φ(x) = (2π)^{-n/2} ∫ φ(t) e^{i x·t} dt` on Schwartz space (DLMF convention 1.14.1, not
  Mathlib's), and `dlmfFourierTempered` (1.16.35) its transpose on `𝓢'`;
* `dlmfD j` (1.16.30), `polyOp` and `polyDiffOp P` (1.16.32), `polyMul P` (1.16.31) on
  Schwartz space, and their transposes `temperedPolyDiffOp P`, `temperedPolyMul P` on `𝓢'`;
* `IsDeltaSequence` (1.17.3), `gaussianDeltaSeq` (1.17.5), `dirichletKernel` (1.17.20).

# Linear second-order differential equations (DLMF §§1.13(i)–1.13(iii))

For `w'' + f(z) w' + g(z) w = r(z)` on an open set `D ⊆ ℂ` with analytic coefficients
(`IsLinODE2SolOn f g r w D`):

* existence of solutions with prescribed `w(z₀)`, `w'(z₀)` on a simply connected domain
  (admitted; proved for entire coefficients from `ODE.exists_entire_solution_linear_ode'`), and
  uniqueness on a connected open set (from `ODE.eqOn_of_ode`);
* linear combinations of solutions, fundamental pairs from initial data 1.13.3 or from a
  nonvanishing Wronskian at one point;
* the two-argument Wronskian as a determinant (1.13.4), Abel's identity 1.13.5 (given a
  primitive `F` of `f`, so that `e^{-∫ f} = e^{-F}`), and the equivalences 1.13.6;
* parameter dependence 1.13.7 (admitted);
* the inhomogeneous equation: existence (admitted), the general solution 1.13.9 and variation
  of parameters 1.13.10 (given primitives of `w₁ r/𝒲` and `w₂ r/𝒲`).

# BBP-type formulas and Gauss's Machin formula (Duverney §8.4)

Writing `1/(mk+j) = ∫₀¹ y^{mk+j-1} dy` and summing the geometric series under the integral sign
gives `∑ aᵏ/(mk+j) = ∫₀¹ y^{j-1}/(1 - a yᵐ) dy` for `0 ≤ a < 1` (`hasSum_pow_div_mul_add`,
Duverney (8.92)–(8.93)). A linear combination of such sums is then the integral of a rational
function, which is evaluated by an explicit primitive. This gives:
* the Bailey–Borwein–Plouffe formula
  `π = ∑ 16^{-k} (4/(8k+1) - 2/(8k+4) - 1/(8k+5) - 1/(8k+6))` (8.90);
* `ln 3 = ∑ 4^{-k}/(2k+1)` and `(3/2) ln 2 = ∑ 9^{-k}/(2k+1)` (8.81)–(8.82);
* `4 ln 5 = ∑ 16^{-k}(4/(4k+1) + 4/(4k+2) + 1/(4k+3))` (8.83);
* `(8/3) ln 3 = ∑ 64^{-k}(4/(6k+2) + 2/(6k+3) + 1/(6k+4))` (Exercise 8.15).

Gauss's Machin-like formula `12 arctan(1/18) + 8 arctan(1/57) - 5 arctan(1/239) = π/4` (8.88)
follows from Mathlib's arctangent addition formula, beside Mathlib's other Machin formulas.

# The alternating harmonic series and Dirichlet's `η` and `λ` (Duverney §2.5)

Mathlib has `ζ(s)` but neither the sum `∑ (-1)^{i}/(i+1) = ln 2` nor the companion series
`η(s) = ∑ (-1)^{n-1} n^{-s}` and `λ(s) = ∑ (2n+1)^{-s}`. This file proves:
* `tendsto_sum_alternating_harmonic`: the partial sums of `∑ (-1)^i/(i+1)` tend to `ln 2`, by
  Abel's limit theorem, after the pattern of Mathlib's Leibniz series for `π`;
* `dirichletLambda_eq`: `λ(s) = (1 - 2^{-s}) ζ(s)` and `dirichletEta_eq`:
  `η(s) = (1 - 2^{1-s}) ζ(s)` for `Re s > 1`, by splitting `ζ` into odd and even terms;
* `tendsto_dirichletEta_one`: `η(1) = ln 2` as a limit of partial sums;
* `tendsto_riemannZeta_atTop`: `ζ(s) → 1` as real `s → ∞`.

# Coulomb functions: definitions (DLMF §33.2)

This is the definitions file of the Coulomb area (DLMF Chapter 33). The functions of
DLMF §33.2 were first defined in the Confluent area (`DLMF.Confluent.Coulomb`,
`DLMF.Confluent.CoulombAsymptotics`, `DLMF.Confluent.CoulombIrregular`); on 2026-10-26 their
definitions were moved down here unchanged, keeping the namespace `DLMF.Confluent`:

* `coulombWave`, `coulombWaveDeriv`: the unnormalized regular solution
  `ρ^{ℓ+1} e^{-iρ} M(ℓ+1-iη, 2ℓ+2, 2iρ)` and its derivative;
* `coulombC`: the normalizing constant `C_ℓ(η)` (DLMF 33.2.5);
* `coulombF`: the regular Coulomb function `F_ℓ(η,ρ)` (DLMF 33.2.4);
* `coulombSigma`, `coulombTheta`: the phase shift `σ_ℓ(η)` (principal branch of the phase) and
  the phase `θ_ℓ(η,ρ)` (DLMF 33.2.9–33.2.10);
* `coulombHPlus`, `coulombHMinus`: the irregular functions `H^±_ℓ(η,ρ)` (DLMF 33.2.8);
* `coulombAPlus`: the constant in `H^+_ℓ = A^+ ρ^{ℓ+1} e^{iρ} U(ℓ+1+iη, 2ℓ+2, -2iρ)`;
* `coulombG`: the irregular function `G_ℓ(η,ρ) = Re H^+_ℓ(η,ρ)` (DLMF 33.2.11).

New Chapter 33 definitions live in the namespace `DLMF.Coulomb`:

* `DLMF.Coulomb.turningPoint`: the outer turning point `ρ_tp(η,ℓ)` (DLMF 33.2.2).

# Tools for Laplace transforms and integrals of exponential and trigonometric integrals

Helper results for DLMF §§6.14–6.15 (`DLMF/ExpIntegrals/ZerosIntegrals.lean`):

* elementary complex Laplace transforms `∫₀^∞ e^{-at} e^{-ct}`, `cos(ut)`, `sin(ut)`;
* the representations `E₁(t) = ∫₁^∞ e^{-tu}/u du`, `Si(t) = ∫₀¹ sin(tu)/u du` and
  `Ein(t) - Cin(t) = ∫₀¹ (cos(tu) - e^{-tu})/u du`;
* Fubini swaps for Laplace transforms of such parametric integrals;
* the closed forms `∫₁^∞ du/((a+u)u) = log(1+a)/a` and
  `∫₀¹ du/(a²+u²) = (π/2 - arctan a)/a` for complex `a` (the latter through the
  principal-branch identity `log((1+ia)/(1-ia)) = log(1+ia) - log(1-ia)`, `Re a > 0`);
* the squared and product integrals of `E₁`, `Ci`, `Si - π/2` (DLMF 6.14.4–6.14.7), by
  integration by parts and explicit antiderivatives;
* for the sums of DLMF §6.15: the Laplace representations of the auxiliary functions `f`, `g`,
  summed geometrically, and evaluated through Binet's formulas, namely the digamma kernel
  integral DLMF 5.9.15 (`DLMF.Gamma.digamma_coth`) and Binet's second formula DLMF 5.9.10
  (`DLMF.Gamma.log_Gamma_binet_arctan`), both from `DLMF/Gamma`.

Helper names carry the prefix `eiTr_`.

# Calculus of the generalized hypergeometric function (Duverney §§3.4–3.6)

For Mathlib's regularized `𝐅(a; b; z) = ∑ ∏(aᵢ)ₙ zⁿ / (n! ∏ Γ(bⱼ+n))`, for all parameters:
* `deriv_regularizedHGFun`: `𝐅'(a; b; z) = (∏ a) 𝐅(a+1; b+1; z)` inside the disc of convergence
  (Duverney Thm 3.11);
* `regularizedHGFun_contiguous`: `θ𝐅(i, a'; b) = i (𝐅(i+1, a'; b) - 𝐅(i, a'; b))`
  (Duverney (3.40));
* `coeff_eq_of_generalized_ode`: a power series solving the generalized hypergeometric equation
  is a multiple of `𝐅` when no `bⱼ` is a nonpositive integer (Duverney Thm 3.8);
* `regularized1F2_one_inhomogeneous_ode`: the exact reciprocal-Gamma forcing
  for `₁F₂(1;b,c;z)`, from Mathlib's regularized coefficients, for every parameter.

# Tools for Legendre integrals

Helper results (prefix `legInt_`) for `DLMF.Legendre.Integrals` and
`DLMF.Legendre.IntegralRepresentations`:

* the `m`-times differentiated Legendre equation for `P_n^{(m)}` and the moment recursion
  `∫ (1-x²)^{m+1} (P_l^{(m+1)})(P_n^{(m+1)}) = (n-m)(n+m+1) ∫ (1-x²)^m P_l^{(m)} P_n^{(m)}`,
  which yields the orthogonality of Ferrers functions of integer degree and order (DLMF 14.17.6);
* Green's identity in the order and the moment recursion behind the weight `1/(1-x²)`
  orthogonality (DLMF 14.17.8);
* the contiguous relation `𝐅(a,b;c) = c 𝐅(a,b;c+1) + a b z 𝐅(a+1,b+1;c+2)` on the cut plane and
  the resulting primitive `d/dw [w^{k+1} 𝐅(a,b;k+2;w)] = w^k 𝐅(a,b;k+1;w)`, which identifies
  iterated integrals of `P_ν` from the endpoint `1` with Ferrers and associated Legendre functions
  of negative integer order (DLMF 14.6.6, 14.6.7);
* analyticity of `z ↦ ∫_0^1 w(s) F(zs) ds` on the cut plane and the resulting cut-plane form of
  the fractional integral DLMF 15.6.8 with `d = 1`, used for DLMF 14.12.5;
* elementary facts on complex powers of positive reals and on the principal slit plane;
* the primitives `g_k = (z²-1)^{k/2} Q^{-k}_ν` with `g_{k+1}' = g_k` and `g_{k+1} → 0` at infinity,
  giving the iterated tail integrals of DLMF 14.6.8 on `(1,∞)` and on the principal sheet;
* integration-by-parts recurrences in the order for the Laplace-type moments
  `∫_0^π w^ν cos(mθ)` and `∫_0^π w^p sin^{2q}θ`, `w = x + √(x²-1) cos θ` (DLMF 14.12.7, 14.12.8);
* Heine's integral `∫_0^∞ dt/(x+√(x²-1) cosh t)^{n+1}` (DLMF 14.12.14) via an explicit primitive
  and a degree recurrence;
* recurrences in degree and order for `∫_0^u (x-√(x²-1) cosh t)^n cosh(mt)`, `u = arcoth x`
  (DLMF 14.12.9), and for `∫_{-1}^1 (1-t²)^n (x-t)^{-p}` (DLMF 14.12.11).

# Connection formulas of the radial Mathieu functions: proofs (DLMF §28.22)

Helpers for `DLMF.Mathieu.Modified.Connection`:

* nonvanishing of `A^{2n}_0`, `A^{2n+1}_1`, `B^{2n+1}_1`, `B^{2n+2}_2` for `q ≠ 0` (from the
  recurrences 28.4.5–28.4.8 and the normalizations 28.4.9–28.4.12);
* the Bessel-product sums `∑ (-1)^ℓ A_{2ℓ} J_ℓ(h)²`, `∑ (-1)^ℓ A_{2ℓ+1} J_ℓ(h) J_{ℓ+1}(h)`,
  `∑ B_{2ℓ+1} J_ℓ(ih) J_{ℓ+1}(ih)` (and the analogue for `se_{2n+2}`), evaluated by the product
  integral 10.9.26, Bessel's integral 10.9.2, Fubini and the integral equations 28.10.1–28.10.8;
  these give the values of `Mc^{(1)}_m`, `Ms^{(1)}_m` at `z = 0` or `z = ∓πi/2` through the
  cross-product series 28.24.2–28.24.5;
* parity and proportionality of `Mc^{(1)}_m`, `Ms^{(1)}_m` to `Ce_m`, `Se_m` (ODE uniqueness);
* the monodromy `z ↦ z + πi` of the second kind, giving 28.22.3–28.22.4.

# The cross-product series of the modified Mathieu functions (proof of DLMF 28.24.1)

* `p2m_floquet_coeff_rec`: integration by parts gives the recurrence 28.14.4 for the exponential
  Fourier coefficients over `[0, π]` of a Floquet solution of Mathieu's equation;
* `p2m_mathieuMeInt_floquet`: `me_k(·, q)` (integer `k`, real `q`) is a Floquet solution with
  multiplier `(-1)^k`;
* `p2m_coeff_facts`: for admissible `(ν, h)` the coefficients `c^ν_{2m}(h²)` solve 28.14.4 with
  `a = λ_ν(h²)` and are bounded;
* `p2m_hasSum_modMathieuTerm`: DLMF 28.24.1, from the abstract theorem `p2m_cross_series`;
* derivatives of the continued Bessel functions in `z` (all orders, `Y` at integer orders by
  uniform limits in the order), locally uniform bounds for the shifted cross-product series
  `p2m_F` (`p2m_fam_locally_uniform`, giving §28.24's locally uniform convergence) and their
  termwise derivatives (`p2m_hasDerivAt_F`);
* `p2m_isModMathieuSolution_modMathieuM`: `M^{(j)}_ν` solves 28.20.1 (`p2m_F_ode`: the second
  derivative is reduced termwise with the Bessel recurrences and summed with 28.14.4);
* the integer-order expansions 28.24.2–28.24.5: the coefficients `c^{±N}_{2k}` as cosine and sine
  integrals of `ce_N`, `se_N` (`p2m_mathieuMeCoeff_ce`, `p2m_mathieuMeCoeff_se`), the parity
  `𝒞_{-k} = (-1)^k 𝒞_k` of the continued cylinder functions, and folding of 28.24.1 over `ℤ`.

# Mathieu functions of noninteger order: expansions, asymptotics, stability (DLMF §§28.15–28.19)

* §28.15: the small-`q` expansions of `λ_ν(q)` (28.15.1), the continued-fraction equation
  28.15.2 and the small-`q` expansion of `me_ν(z, q)` (28.15.3);
* §28.16: the large-`q` asymptotic expansion 28.16.1 of `λ_ν(h²)`;
* §28.17: stability of the pairs `(a, q)` (`DLMF.Mathieu.IsMathieuStable`);
* §28.18 only refers to §28.28 (part C of the Chapter 28 acquisition): no statements;
* §28.19: expansions of `π`-pseudoperiodic functions in series of `me_{ν+2n}`.

The expansions with printed finitely many terms followed by `⋯` are stated as `O`-estimates of
the remainder after the printed terms (`q → 0` in `ℂ`, resp. `h → +∞`). All formulas were
checked numerically (truncated tridiagonal eigenproblems for the recurrence 28.14.4, scripts in
the acquisition scratch directory); 28.16.1 was checked for `ν = 0.3, 0.5, 1.5, 2.4` and
`h = 10, 20` (remainder ratios consistent with `O(h⁻⁶)`).

